n: Proceedings of the IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pp. 1053–1061. (2018)\nL114: \nL115:   9. Dang, H., Liu, F., Stehouwer, J., Liu, X., Jain, A.K.: On the detection of digital face manipulation. In: Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pp. 5781–5790. (2020)\nL116:   10. Xi, Z., Huang, W., Wei, K., Luo, W., Zheng, P.: AI-generated image detection using a cross-attention enhanced dual-stream network. In: 2023 Asia Pacific Signal and Information Processing Association Annual Summit and Conference (APSIPA ASC), pp. 1463–1470. IEEE (2023)\nL117: \nL118:   11. Zhang, R.S., Quan, W.Z., Fan, L.B., Hu, L.M., Yan, D.M.: Distinguishing computer-generated images from natural images using channel and pixel correlation. J. Comput. Sci. Technol. 35, 592–602 (2020)\nL119:   12. Gangan, M.P., Anoop, K., Lajish, V.L.: Distinguishing natural and computer generated images using multi-colorspace fused efficientNet. J. Inf. Secur. Appl. 68, 103261 (2022)\nL120: \nL121:   13. Bird, J.J., Lotfi, A.: Cifake: image classification and explainable identification of AI-generated synthetic images. IEEE Access 12, 15642–15650 (2024)\nL122:   14. Zhu, M., Chen, H., Yan, Q., Huang, X., Lin, G., Li, W., Tu, Z., Hu, H., Hu, J., Wang, Y.: GenImage: a million-scale benchmark for detecting AI-generated image. Adv. Neural Inf. Process. Syst. (NeurIPS) 36, 77771 (2023)\nL123: \nL124:   15. Meena, K.B., Tyagi, V.: Distinguishing computer-generated images from photographic images using two-stream convolutional neural network. Appl. Soft Comput. 100, 107025 (2021)\nL125:   16. Nataraj, L., Mohammed, T. M., Chandrasekaran, S., Flenner, A., Bappy, J. H., Roy-Chowdhury, A.K., Manjunath, B.S.: Detecting GAN generated fake images using co-occurrence matrices (2019). arXiv preprint 【30†arXiv:1903.06836†arxiv.org】\nL126: \nL127:   17. Hsu, C.C., Zhuang, Y.X., Lee, C.Y.: Deep fake image detection based on pairwise learning. Appl. Sci. 10(1), 370 (2020)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetecting violent deepfakes: dataset and a compact attention network with multi-scale supervision | Machine Vision and Applications | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00138-026-01836-7)\nciteturn213view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00138-026-01836-7\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 349\nL0: Account\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Menu】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Find a journal】 【2†Publish with us†www.springernature.com】 【3†Track your research†link.springernature.com】\nL5: \nL6: 【4†Search】\nL7: \nL8: 【5†Saved research】\nL9: \nL10: 【6†Cart†order.springer.com】\nL11: # Detecting violent deepfakes: dataset and a compact attention network with multi-scale supervision\nL12: \nL13:   * Research\nL14:   * Published: 30 May 2026\nL15: \nL16:   * Volume 37, article number 79 (2026)\nL17:   * 【7†Cite this article】\nL18: \nL19: [Input] Save article\nL20: \nL21: 【5†View saved research】\nL22: \nL23: 【8†Machine Vision and Applications】 【9†Aims and scope】 【10†Submit manuscript†submission.springernature.com】\nL24: \nL25:   * 【11†Surbhi Raj】^{【12†1】},\nL26:   * 【13†Jimson Mathew】^{【12†1】} &\nL27:   * 【14†Arijit Mondal】^{【12†1】}\nL28: \nL29:   * 144 Accesses\nL30: \nL31:   * 【15†Explore all metrics】\nL32: ## Abstract\nL33: Violent deepfake images could be used in propaganda campaigns that aim to mislead the public and cause harm in the real world. Safeguarding information in the modern digital age requires detecting and disclosing violent deepfake images. The application of deepfake technology raises serious ethical concerns, especially when used to produce harmful or violent media. Recently, Diffusion Models (DMs) have emerged as a highly promising approach for synthesizing images.\nL34: These models surpass Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) in both diversity and quality, demonstrating strong performance across tasks such as text-to-image and image-to-image modeling. Diffusion models can produce highly realistic fake violent images, spreading misinformation and potentially inciting violence.\nL35: This study proposes a compact attention network with 0.43 million parameters that leverages the two-channel, multi-scale frequency and noise features of an input image to distinguish between fake and real violent images. We also introduce a new dataset consisting of 34,695 violent images, containing 17,442 fake violent images generated by a Stable Diffusion model and 17,253 real violent images collected via the Google Image Search API based on visual attributes such as protest, fire, blood, injuries, etc.\nL36: Using this dataset, we train our model and demonstrate its performance. It achieves 98% accuracy and 97.8% average precision on the newly proposed Deepfake Violent Image Dataset (DVID) of violent images, including both fake and real ones. Additionally, the proposed neural architecture is tested for detecting DM-generated images to assess its generalizability and robustness.\nL37: We present experimental findings on the DiffusionForensics benchmark dataset for cross-DM and cross-color transformations of variants of diffusion model-generated images. This study also exhibits that the proposed model achieves comparable accuracy with fewer parameters and faster inference time. The code and dataset can be accessed at 【16†https://github.com/surbhiraj786/Deepfake-Violent-Detection.git†github.com】.\nL38: This is a preview of subscription content, to check access.\nL39: ## Access this article\nL40: \nL41: Log in via an institution\nL42: \nL43: ## Subscribe and save\nL44: \nL45: Springer+\nL46: \nL47: from $39.99 /Month\nL48: \nL49:   * Starting from 10 chapters or articles per month\nL50:   * Access and download chapters and articles from more than 300k books and 2,500 journals\nL51:   * Cancel anytime\nL52: 【17†View plans】\nL53: ## Buy Now\nL54: \nL5